    "two pointers",
    "sorting"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "you already know how to turn 3Sum's triple loop into fix-one-plus-two-pointers. what would it take to peel off two fixed elements instead of one?",
    "sort first, then use two nested loops to fix the first two numbers, and two pointers to find the remaining pair - same two-pointer core as 3Sum, just wrapped in one more loop",
    "skip duplicate values at every level (both fixed loops and both pointers) to avoid repeating a quadruplet",
    "watch for integer overflow if your language uses fixed-size integers when summing four large numbers"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "not skipping duplicates at every level, producing repeated quadruplets",
    "not sorting first, which breaks the two-pointer step entirely",
    "using four nested loops (or four-deep recursion) instead of reducing the innermost two to two pointers"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of integers and a target.\n\n**Find every set of four distinct positions whose values add up to the target, and return the list of such value quadruplets.**\n\nNo duplicate quadruplet should appear in the result, even if it can be formed from different positions.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 200",
      "-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
      "-4 * 10^9 <= target <= 4 * 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "fewer than four elements (answer is empty)",
      "many repeated values",
      "no quadruplet sums to target (answer is empty)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
